record akr.evidence.one-writer-at-a-time-holds-20260908/1 : evidence {
    title "Six concurrent writers all succeed and all six records survive"
    state verified
    result pass
    method command
    observed_at git:0dd6057aafb21ef86ae8d2bc7a659ed3c7fd1ebc
    command "akr init in a throwaway repo, then six concurrent 'akr evidence add' against one workspace"
    summary """
        Re-ran the reproduction from
        @akr.observation.concurrent-writes-are-silently-lost on 2026-09-08 against the installed
        0.5.0 binary. Six  processes started together against one fresh workspace:
        all six printed a created line and exited 0, and all six records are present in the ledger
        (grep -c '^record ' returns 6; race-1 through race-6 each found). .akr/cache/write.lock is
        created, which is the advisory lock @akr.decision.one-writer-at-a-time specifies. Under the
        behaviour the observation recorded, two of the six would have survived.

        The same defect had been logged independently in a sister ledger as
        kitchen.papercut.concurrent-akr-evidence-add-processes-both (2026-08-11) and was retested
        there the same day with the same outcome. Worth recording that this ledger-cleanup run was
        itself nine agents writing to fifteen workspaces in parallel: the condition the observation
        describes is exactly the condition the run created, and it held.
        """
}

